WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.192 --> 00:00:02.917
The sky is falling! The sky is falling!

00:00:02.917 --> 00:00:06.094
Narrator: See how these trees
sway like one single blob

00:00:06.094 --> 00:00:07.950
in Disney's "Chicken Little"?

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.891
Compare that to this tree in "Encanto,"

00:00:10.891 --> 00:00:12.780
where each individual leaf

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.540
moves realistically on its own.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.370
In fact, zoom out on "Chicken Little,"

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.520
and you'll see just how little
detail is in the background

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.867
compared to Disney's newer features.

00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:25.140
"Chicken Little" was Disney's first foray

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.240
into full 3D animation.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.840
So the worlds the studio could
create were a lot simpler.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.890
But animated worlds have
evolved a lot since 2005.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.168
And one film in particular kicked off

00:00:37.168 --> 00:00:40.477
a whole new era of realistic environments:

00:00:40.477 --> 00:00:42.037
"Tangled."

00:00:42.037 --> 00:00:44.555
Steve: "Tangled" really represented to me

00:00:44.555 --> 00:00:46.533
the film where we achieved

00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:48.925
the promise of taking the artistry

00:00:48.925 --> 00:00:51.390
that existed at Disney Animation Studios

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.000
and applied that to the medium of CG.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.770
Narrator: To create
the world of "Tangled,"

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.690
or any 3D-animated world,

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.600
Disney has a dedicated environments team,

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.633
whose job is to model and
develop the movie's backdrop

00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:05.640
based on the concept designs.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.950
The studio then uses software programs

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.310
to automatically simulate the
behavior of world elements,

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:12.780
like plants, water,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.030
and lighting that illuminates it all.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.550
To fill up the whimsical
forests of "Tangled,"

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.250
Disney developed special
tree-modeling tools

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.507
to help the environments team

00:01:21.507 --> 00:01:24.780
lay down the geometry for lots of trees,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.060
all with a specific fairy-tale look

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.860
that echoed the flowing shapes

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.560
of hand-drawn animation and painting.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.770
These new tree tools included
a program called Skeletor,

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.560
which let artists hand-model 3D geometry

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.270
on certain hero trees,

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.700
like this one that Rapunzel swings from.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.730
They also built a new
software called Dendro,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:48.063
which let them art-direct the
growth of the whimsical trees

00:01:48.063 --> 00:01:50.760
and program the direction
and amplitude of wind

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.950
in the trees' branches and leaves.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.625
Dendro also helped calculate
how branches should grow

00:01:55.625 --> 00:01:57.510
according to environmental factors

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.935
like gravity, light,
and natural obstacles.

00:02:00.935 --> 00:02:02.850
The storybook trees for "Frozen"

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.310
were even more stylized than "Tangled,"

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.270
inspired by the unusual tree
design in "Sleeping Beauty."

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.340
To create these highly specific shapes

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.050
and branching structures,

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.960
Disney developed a full
vegetation generator

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.873
to replace Dendro, called Bonsai.

00:02:18.873 --> 00:02:22.195
With Bonsai, the studio
could build a wide library

00:02:22.195 --> 00:02:25.290
of trees, bushes, plants, and foliage

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.150
that could be shared and reused

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.900
across other Disney
productions, like "Big Hero 6."

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.025
Bonsai came in handy for
animated vegetation like this

00:02:34.025 --> 00:02:36.690
in the film's setting of San Fransokyo.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.930
The main challenge was
its fully urban landscape,

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.122
which stood apart from
the organic environments

00:02:42.122 --> 00:02:43.680
seen in earlier films,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.950
involving many more layers
of objects, movement,

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.260
and, above all, lights.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.135
Disney needed a way to render
complex lighting environments.

00:02:53.135 --> 00:02:55.380
Luckily, Disney engineers
were hard at work

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.320
on a powerful new
renderer called Hyperion,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.390
which could produce densely packed scenes

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:02.970
with light bouncing
around in every direction,

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:06.330
handling several million rays at a time.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.940
This allowed the team
to build a vibrant city,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.050
lit in some shots

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.510
by hundreds of thousands of sources,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.180
from streetlamps to car headlights.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.370
That was a big leap in
terms of complexity,

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.830
in terms of the number
of individual assets

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.620
that we can actually render in one scene.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.728
Narrator: It also helped
the filmmakers on "Moana"

00:03:24.728 --> 00:03:27.990
capture the fanciful sense
of scale they wanted.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:29.940
The name of Moana's fictional home

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.670
means, literally, "big island."

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.840
And thanks to Hyperion,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.180
the team could produce
sunlit shots of the beach

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.330
where water simulations with
full surface reflections

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.000
extended a quarter mile into the distance

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.327
and village shots with about 10 times

00:03:44.327 --> 00:03:46.999
the geometric complexity of San Fransokyo.

00:03:46.999 --> 00:03:49.620
The renderer also powered over 100 shots

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.240
of the monster Te Kā,

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.550
illuminated by bright streams of lava

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.130
permeating through massive smoke plumes.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:58.620
To shape the behavior of the molten lava,

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.980
Disney updated its physically
based simulator Matterhorn,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.680
originally developed for
the snow in "Frozen."

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.540
Disney now expanded the software

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.480
to handle the more
viscous substance of lava,

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.670
along with other natural island materials

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.920
like sand, mud, and foam.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.110
A scouting trip to Tahiti helped Larry,

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.180
the head of environments for "Moana,"

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:19.860
determine how each of the film's

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.260
over 60 species of vegetation

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.060
should move with the wind.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:25.830
Every minute, everything was moving.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.573
So we knew we had to do that in our movie.

00:04:27.573 --> 00:04:29.850
Narrator: Like the fact
that coconut trees' leaflets

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.130
and bows should sway with the breeze

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.620
while the trunks themselves remain still.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.580
For more dramatic vegetation movement

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.580
in the Te Fiti healing sequence,

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:41.130
the effects team created
spreading texture maps

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.300
of plant growth.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.479
You could almost claim
that "Moana" is probably

00:04:44.479 --> 00:04:46.901
the real progenesis of
when we started to do

00:04:46.901 --> 00:04:49.881
really complex animated
vegetation at the studio.

00:04:49.881 --> 00:04:52.758
Narrator: Of course, the
star of this world was water,

00:04:52.758 --> 00:04:55.020
present in nearly every shot

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.150
and in much more complex forms

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.040
than Disney had ever produced,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.930
including waves of all sizes

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:04.230
breaking in specific patterns
on the sand and reef.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:07.260
So Disney developed a
new engine called Splash,

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.210
which could simulate waves running,

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:10.770
crashing, and breaking

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.650
and create secondary
effects like boat wakes.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.990
But while the water in "Moana" looks warm,

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.060
crystal-clear, and inviting,

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.100
the Dark Sea in "Frozen II"

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.230
had to be rough, dark, and stormy

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.030
to convey the threats facing Elsa

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.010
as she embarks on her quest.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.110
And I feel like we just continually

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:30.180
stand on our own shoulders

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:31.860
with the films that come before us.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.980
So, creating the waves in "Frozen II,"

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.722
that would've been a whole
different adventure for us

00:05:36.722 --> 00:05:39.240
if "Moana" hadn't come before that.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:40.890
Narrator: So the team
shot reference footage

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:43.230
in parts of the Southern California coast

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:45.780
where the waves break especially hard.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.640
They then used a set of equations

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.100
to model the motion of the Dark Sea waves,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.960
experimenting with different values

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.268
for variables like amplitude
and ocean-floor depth

00:05:55.268 --> 00:05:56.340
until they got waves

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:59.370
that were three to four times Elsa's size.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.380
The artists then added more foam

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:03.857
and spindrift mist to capture the rough,

00:06:03.857 --> 00:06:06.090
churned-up nature of the Dark Sea.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:09.814
But the Dark Sea only marks
one stage in Elsa's quest.

00:06:09.814 --> 00:06:11.160
Most of the film takes place

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.320
in another entirely new setting,

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:16.530
the Enchanted Forest, far from Arendelle.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:19.140
Just as the waves had to tower over Elsa,

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:20.760
making her look small,

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.585
many of the shots in the
forest are framed extra-wide

00:06:23.585 --> 00:06:25.980
to emphasize the characters' isolation,

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.980
which also puts more of
the world on display.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.497
And since the characters
spend so much time

00:06:31.497 --> 00:06:33.363
in this setting, Disney had to take

00:06:33.363 --> 00:06:36.491
its forest-population
capabilities up a notch.

00:06:36.491 --> 00:06:40.500
So it developed a new
vegetation-placement tool, Droplet.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.727
Designed as an interactive brush,

00:06:42.727 --> 00:06:45.168
Droplet let artists paint down, scale,

00:06:45.168 --> 00:06:48.493
and reposition vegetation
in an art-directed way,

00:06:48.493 --> 00:06:50.250
giving them control over the flow

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.350
and style of the forest as a whole.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:55.067
The artists had to paint
not only the canopy,

00:06:55.067 --> 00:06:56.700
but also the ground cover

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.920
in the saturated colors of autumn

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.050
in contrast to the snow-blanketed grounds

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:02.730
of the first movie.

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:05.400
Luckily, they could use
Bonsai from "Frozen I"

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:08.583
to manipulate the color of
the leaves on the forest floor

00:07:08.583 --> 00:07:10.380
and to make the forest look striking

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:13.805
not just in overhead shots,
but from every angle.

00:07:13.805 --> 00:07:16.245
This forest is beautiful.

00:07:16.245 --> 00:07:18.605
Narrator: Since the snow
has melted in Arendelle,

00:07:18.605 --> 00:07:20.520
the team had to render its buildings

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.260
in much higher detail,

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:24.750
increasing the texture resolution.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.390
This visual richness was equally necessary

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:28.890
for the forest dam,

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.898
where surface textures
helped tell the backstory.

00:07:31.898 --> 00:07:34.748
The filmmakers decided
that King Runeard's workers

00:07:34.748 --> 00:07:36.000
would have to cut the stone

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.830
from the surrounding fjord walls

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:40.080
and assemble each block by hand.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.400
To reinforce this idea,

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.208
artists sculpted in high-level detail

00:07:44.208 --> 00:07:47.100
little imperfections on all the surfaces

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:48.510
and used texturing software

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.550
to create subtle material variation,

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.841
like these uniquely rotated
cut marks on each block.

00:07:53.841 --> 00:07:57.395
And to show that 34 years had
passed since its construction,

00:07:57.395 --> 00:08:00.057
artists used Droplet to
paint hundreds of thousands

00:08:00.057 --> 00:08:03.150
of iterations of moss, grass, and mulch.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:06.100
Disney continued to improve
that vegetation pipeline

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:08.160
for "Raya and the Last Dragon."

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.680
But the major focus was this:

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:14.070
the atmospherics of the
Southeast Asian settings.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:17.580
These regions tend to be
dense with water and moisture,

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:18.850
so the environments called for

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:20.910
a lot of volume-based elements,

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:23.430
like morning mist and ground fog.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:25.000
Traditionally, that work would fall

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.756
on the effects department, but in "Raya,"

00:08:27.756 --> 00:08:29.523
instead of having the effects department

00:08:29.523 --> 00:08:32.587
create atmospheric elements
on a per-shot basis,

00:08:32.587 --> 00:08:35.280
Disney created these
elements in preproduction

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.190
and put them into an extensive library.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:40.899
Later, as lighting artists
were lighting a shot,

00:08:40.899 --> 00:08:42.390
they could pull from that library

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:45.000
and compose the atmospherics into it

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.620
without having to request new assets

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:48.750
from the effects department every time.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:50.580
This gave the lighters more flexibility

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:52.980
over complex atmospheric setups

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.378
and more freedom to express

00:08:54.378 --> 00:08:56.820
the emotional story in each scene.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:00.630
Dust and smoke signified moments
of conflict and misgivings,

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:02.520
while trusting moments between characters

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.180
were heavier on water
elements like mist and steam.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:08.310
Just as "Raya"'s Southeast Asian setting

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.963
defined its world elements,

00:09:09.963 --> 00:09:13.022
"Encanto"'s Colombian setting
called for a lush jungle

00:09:13.022 --> 00:09:15.150
surrounding the Madrigals' village.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.675
And this environment had to be in motion,

00:09:17.675 --> 00:09:20.412
like we'd see more of later
on with "Strange World,"

00:09:20.412 --> 00:09:23.250
which was in production at
the same time as "Encanto."

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.833
Specifically, Isabela's
song is brought to life

00:09:25.833 --> 00:09:28.050
by the whole animated environment,

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:29.430
with these flowering plants

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.530
growing in sync with the music.

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:35.555
But that dynamic environment
work was done mostly by hand.

00:09:35.555 --> 00:09:37.741
Ben: Each artist had to go
into each and every shot

00:09:37.741 --> 00:09:39.840
of that sequence, which
was a beautiful sequence,

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.783
and bring in custom rigs that allowed them

00:09:42.783 --> 00:09:45.210
to do the growth for those plants.

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:46.470
Narrator: Disney had to figure out a way

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:49.800
to do that work in advance
for "Strange World,"

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:53.160
which, more than any other
Disney animated environment,

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:56.998
featured a lot of moving parts, literally.

00:09:56.998 --> 00:09:59.010
Oh, wow.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:00.690
It's beautiful!

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.100
Narrator: Elements like these plants

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.960
acted as background characters

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:06.843
and part of the environment
at the same time.

00:10:06.843 --> 00:10:09.120
To set the whole landscape in motion,

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:12.210
the studio created an entirely new role,

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:14.370
animated-environments supervisor.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:16.560
Ben: That role has me interacting

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.050
with almost every
department in the studio,

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:21.540
certainly with the
production environments team,

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:23.970
to ensure that the assets
that they're building

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:25.830
will be animatable,

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:27.570
and then that would move towards

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:29.370
effects and character animation

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:31.350
to provide animation for those plants,

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:33.990
kind of ferrying the moving plants

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:36.450
through the whole pipeline
one shot at a time.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.370
Narrator: Disney also
adjusted its pipeline

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.380
so that Ben's team could fine-tune

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.264
the environments' movement
earlier on in the process.

00:10:43.264 --> 00:10:46.427
Ben: New to this show was
the opportunity to say,

00:10:46.427 --> 00:10:48.842
"That plant right there is
moving a little too much,"

00:10:48.842 --> 00:10:50.640
or, "It's just a little too crazy,"

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.620
to make sure that we were handing off

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:55.503
the plants and the
environment in its best state

00:10:55.503 --> 00:10:58.383
before it hit the really
render-intensive departments

00:10:58.383 --> 00:10:59.910
such as effects and lighting,

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.651
where things start to get a
little bit harder to change.

00:11:02.651 --> 00:11:04.680
So we would do the growing in advance,

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.120
and then when shots came,

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:07.770
we would place that animation in.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:10.712
So all of the work was
front-loaded for "Strange World,"

00:11:10.712 --> 00:11:13.293
which allowed us to do
many hundreds more shots

00:11:13.293 --> 00:11:14.850
than "Encanto" did.

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:16.170
Narrator: The environments in this movie

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:19.258
weren't just dynamic. They
were some of the largest

00:11:19.258 --> 00:11:21.630
ever created at Disney Animation,

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:25.080
with some stretching 2 ½ miles wide,

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:28.755
so large that the team needed
to expand its computing power.

00:11:28.755 --> 00:11:30.664
Ben: At the peak, I think there was a shot

00:11:30.664 --> 00:11:34.560
with 20 or 30 million
blades of alien grass.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:37.201
Narrator: To convey the
Strange World's vastness,

00:11:37.201 --> 00:11:39.090
the lighting team made heavy use

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:42.332
of atmospheric elements,
like those used for "Raya,"

00:11:42.332 --> 00:11:44.130
adding clouds and ground fog

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:46.620
to create a sense of depth and scale.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:48.240
These expansive sets

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:51.060
lent themselves to wider camera lenses.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:52.320
The camera and layout team

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:55.500
could use controls to
adjust the lens distortion.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:56.820
So whenever they wanted a shot

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.510
to feel off-kilter and, well, strange,

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:02.970
they could dial up the distortion effect,

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:06.453
a decision always driven by
the story context of the scene.

00:12:06.453 --> 00:12:08.580
It all comes together with editing,

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:10.170
which gives the audience space

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.030
to take in the environment,

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:14.220
and sound design, which helps communicate

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:15.893
the character of the space.

00:12:15.893 --> 00:12:18.300
[buzzing and crackling]

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:19.825
Sarah: Because, you know,
it doesn't have a face,

00:12:19.825 --> 00:12:21.030
it can't communicate that way,

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:22.770
but it can communicate through sound.

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:24.990
It's squishy, it breathes.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.660
I make sure that there's
an intentional personality

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.601
behind every little sound
that the plants make

00:12:30.601 --> 00:12:33.330
as they breathe in or
breathe out or undulate.

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:36.088
Narrator: Now that Disney's
built such a powerful tool set

00:12:36.088 --> 00:12:38.179
for living, breathing worlds,

00:12:38.179 --> 00:12:40.560
the team has to step it back sometimes,

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:43.290
especially since, unlike
live-action-film makers,

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:45.690
they're creating these
worlds from the ground up.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:48.275
In feature animation, sort
of the sky's the limit,

00:12:48.275 --> 00:12:51.210
and you can easily put
more in than you need.

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:52.890
You're looking for that perfect blend

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:56.363
of organic, fascinating
texture in the background,

00:12:56.363 --> 00:12:58.650
not so much that it's going to take away

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:00.210
from what the characters are doing,

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:01.950
what the characters are saying.

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:03.620
Narrator: After all, Disney builds worlds

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.870
to service the story,
not the other way around.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:07.772
For the same reason,

00:13:07.772 --> 00:13:11.190
it doesn't stick to any
house style for world design,

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:14.220
instead tailoring each
world to its story needs.

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:16.590
Just contrast the details of the castles

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:18.360
in "Tangled" and "Frozen."

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:20.643
Steve: Rapunzel's tower, the wooden beams,

00:13:20.643 --> 00:13:22.410
there isn't a straight beam anywhere.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:24.668
Everything's handcrafted, hand-hewn,

00:13:24.668 --> 00:13:27.000
everything's got a slight twist

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.250
and an organic feel to it,

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:32.040
riffing off of the design principles

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:34.740
that applied to "Snow
White" and "Pinocchio."

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:38.010
When you look at the interiors
of the castle in "Frozen,"

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.830
we do have a lot of very
straight, rigid lines,

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.875
and that's, of course, all done to support

00:13:43.875 --> 00:13:45.760
what we're saying about
those environments.

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:47.430
Oh, this is for royalty.

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:49.380
OK, they were getting the best craftsmen,

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:50.940
who could make the straightest line.

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.430
And really everything needs to go through

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:56.250
that level of rigor and discussion.

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:58.170
Narrator: Those decisions
on "Strange World"

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:00.820
struck a balance between
making the landscape

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:03.633
feel imaginative and unexpected

00:14:03.633 --> 00:14:06.030
while retaining the story-first principle

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:09.239
that made the classic
Disney movies so beloved.

